# The second rejection hands an issue to a human; the label it leaves behind says
# it is still queued.
# Prepared here rather than landed, for the reason every other file in
# `docs/ci/` gives: `.github/` is copied from the agent-pipeline template and the
# pipeline's push token has no `workflow` scope, so a branch editing
# `.github/workflows/agent-review.yml` fails at the end of a PR rather than at
# the start of it.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-review-reject-stuck-label.patch
#
# and that is the whole change. One line.
#
# What it changes. The `Reject` step's `MAX_REJECTIONS` branch adds
# `agent:stuck` and nothing else, so an issue that has been rejected twice ends
# up carrying `agent:stuck` *and* the `agent:queued` its first rejection added.
# `docs/agent-pipeline.md` says the second rejection "gets `agent:stuck`
# instead", and the comment this branch posts tells a human to remove
# `agent:stuck` and add `agent:queued` -- an instruction that is wrong
# precisely because `agent:queued` is already there. The one line makes both
# true and changes no prose anywhere.
#
# **It is a `local` hunk, not a template one, so unlike the gate patches in this
# directory it is not at risk from a re-copy.** The `Reject` step and the third
# `rejected` verdict are this repository's own (`LOCAL CHANGE (2026-09-24, not
# in the agent-pipeline template)` in the file's header), so the context this
# hunk needs is context this repository wrote. The `agent-gates-*.patch` files
# cut `agent-gates.sh`, which *is* copied, and their failure mode is a re-copy
# moving the lines a hunk was cut against; this one has no such source. The
# corollary is that this patch is not competing for an insertion point with
# them, and unlike them there is no free-anchor problem to solve: a hunk that
# replaces one line needs no anchor at all.
#
# Why the combined `--add-label ... --remove-label ...` form rather than a
# second `gh issue edit` line. It is the form the other hand-offs in this tree
# already use -- the stuck branches of `agent-fix.yml` and `agent-implement.yml`
# -- and it is one API call that cannot half-apply. The comment above the two
# `|| true` removals at the foot of this step gives the reason a *separate*
# removal is the awkward form here, and it is worth reading before anyone
# re-cuts this: it records that these labels are alternatives ("an issue carries
# one of these, not both") and that removing a label the issue does not carry is
# an error, which is why those two are guarded. `agent:queued` is on the issue
# by the time this branch runs -- the `else` branch below is what put it there
# on the first rejection -- so its removal is not the absent-label case, but it
# is unguarded anyway, which is only safe because it is fused to the add.
#
# What it does not change. It does not touch `MAX_REJECTIONS`, so an issue is
# still planned twice before a human is handed it. It does not touch the
# `else` branch, so a first rejection still queues the issue exactly as
# before. And it changes no consumer: every sweep and triage that reads these
# labels already filters `agent:stuck` out, so nothing here alters which issues
# the pipeline picks up -- only what a human sees on the issue.
#
# Added 2026-10-03, issue #410. The write-up is
# `docs/findings/review-reject-verdict-contract.md`, which also holds the
# committed check on the four approved/rejected combinations. Nothing about
# this patch has been run: no rejection has been performed and no label has
# been observed on a live issue, so the claim here is about the committed text
# and what `git apply --check` says about it, nothing more.

diff --git a/.github/workflows/agent-review.yml b/.github/workflows/agent-review.yml
index d002c9a..2f376de 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/agent-review.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/agent-review.yml
@@ -421,7 +421,7 @@ jobs:
 
           if [ "$rejections" -ge "$MAX_REJECTIONS" ]; then
             gh issue comment "$ISSUE" --body "PR #${PR} was rejected, and that is rejection ${rejections} of this issue, so it is being handed to a human rather than planned again. Remove the \`agent:stuck\` label and add \`agent:queued\` to let the pipeline try once more. <!-- agent-rejected -->"
-            gh issue edit "$ISSUE" --add-label 'agent:stuck'
+            gh issue edit "$ISSUE" --add-label 'agent:stuck' --remove-label 'agent:queued'
           else
             gh issue comment "$ISSUE" --body "PR #${PR} was rejected (rejection ${rejections} of ${MAX_REJECTIONS}). The review is now at the top of this issue, and the retry sweep will plan it again from here. <!-- agent-rejected -->"
             gh issue edit "$ISSUE" --add-label 'agent:queued'
